# Valgrind suppressions for the constant-time harness (ct_flows, ct.yml).
#
# Policy (see SECURITY_TESTING.md, "Constant-time verification"): a suppression is allowed only
# for a KNOWN, ACCEPTED pattern inside a dependency that we cannot fix at our
# boundary, and every entry must carry a justification. Anything not listed
# here, or in the default suppressions Valgrind loads for system libraries,
# fails the run. The one pattern below is P-256-only; the
# curve25519-dalek paths run without any suppression.
#
# Written against p256 0.14.0 / primeorder 0.14.0 / hash2curve 0.14.0 /
# subtle 2.6.1 (rustc 1.99.1, Valgrind 3.22, x86_64 Linux, debug and
# release) and first verified on 2026-10-19 in a run with `-v`; the debug
# entry's glob was narrowed after that. Every ct.yml run checks both entries:
# Valgrind's `-s` lists how many errors each one absorbed, and
# ci/check-ct-suppressions.py fails the run unless that list is exactly the
# one valgrind-ct.expected gives for the build.
# Re-triage when bumping these crates or on suppression misses after a
# toolchain drift (ct.yml runs unpinned @stable by design, to catch codegen
# that turns constant-time source leaky). A miss fails the run, and so does
# a count that moves; take the new frames from a run with
# `--gen-suppressions=all`, which prints them in the form they are matched in.
#
# How an entry matches. Valgrind compares `fun:` lines with *mangled* names,
# not the demangled ones its log prints: an outlined frame by its raw symbol,
# in which rustc's v0 mangling length-prefixes every identifier, and an
# inlined frame by its bare DWARF name, which the log does print. An inlined
# frame whose origin Valgrind cannot resolve is printed, and matched, as the
# literal `UnknownInlinedFun`. Valgrind also commons errors up by four code
# addresses, the place in the erring function and in each of its three
# callers (its manual, under `--num-callers`). An inlined frame shares the
# address of the function it is inlined into and adds none, so errors whose
# top four printed frames match can still be counted apart. A later error
# with the same four addresses is counted under the first one's record,
# suppressed or not, wherever it comes from. An entry that reaches beneath
# its pattern (a leading `...`), or whose justification holds only for some
# callers, therefore covers more than it says. Each entry below is the
# pattern's own two frames, innermost first, with no `...`, so only that
# pattern can match it or join its errors; everything else, the rest of the
# hash-to-curve and all of sec1 included, runs unsuppressed.
#
# The pattern: p256's hash-to-curve branches on a subtle::CtOption
# discriminant that is invariant-true by construction, in
# `tv4.invert().unwrap()` in the OSSWU map (primeorder 0.14.0, src/osswu.rs:146,
# the only `unwrap` in that file). tv4 = A * CMOV(Z, -tv2, tv2 != 0) is never
# zero for P-256 (A = -3, Z = -10), so the inversion always succeeds, yet
# `CtOption::unwrap` still executes a hard `assert!` on that (tainted)
# always-true flag. The condition never varies with the secret input, so no
# timing signal exists; the taint is by design (the hash-to-curve input is the
# password, or the private input of an RFC 9497 OPRF / VOPRF / POPRF).
# Dependency-internal; cannot be declassified at our boundary. The assert is a
# branch, so the entries are for Cond, the only kind reported for it.
# The second frame is `<FieldElement as OsswuMap>::osswu`, whose symbol
# contains `...8OsswuMap5osswu`; the length prefix keeps the glob off
# `AffineOsswuMap`'s `osswu`, the function that calls it.

# Release: `CtOption<FieldElement>::unwrap` is inlined into the outlined
# `osswu` and matches by its bare name. The frames above it may have no name:
# the 2026-10-19 build inlined the RFC 9497 suite's hash-to-curve into generic
# callers, and Valgrind printed the frames inlined between them and
# `map_to_curve` as `UnknownInlinedFun`, so the entry does not anchor on them.
{
   p256-hash2curve-invariant-ctoption-assert-osswu-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:unwrap<*FieldElement>
   fun:*8OsswuMap5osswu*
}

# Debug: both frames are outlined and match by their symbols. `unwrap`'s is
# `subtle::CtOption<p256::...::FieldElement>::unwrap`, which contains
# `...6subtle...8CtOption...12FieldElementE6unwrap`: the `E` that closes the
# generic arguments just before `6unwrap` keeps the glob off a
# `...16unwrap_unchecked` and off `Option<CtOption<FieldElement>>::unwrap`.
{
   p256-hash2curve-invariant-ctoption-assert-osswu-outlined-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*6subtle*8CtOption*12FieldElementE6unwrap*
   fun:*8OsswuMap5osswu*
}

# Until 0.6.1 this file also held broad entries, anchored on a call with a
# leading `...`: this assert under `hash_from_bytes`, whose `...` also reached
# the rest of the hash-to-curve, the field inversion included, and sec1's tag
# dispatch under `P256Group::to_bytes` (before that, three entries for sec1's
# compressed encoder as well). Every P-256 encoding in pakery-crypto now goes
# through a constant-time encoder, so no sec1 encoder runs on secret data. The
# broad entries also covered more than their call: in the debug build, where
# dependency code is outlined, every later tag dispatch through sec1's
# `as_bytes` had the same top four addresses as the first, all of them in
# sec1, and was counted under the sec1 entry whatever called it.
